/* ============================================================================
   three-linear-equations.epher -- 3x3 system via rref on the augmented matrix

   A system of three linear equations in x, y and z is solved by Gaussian
   elimination: put the coefficients and the right side into the augmented
   matrix and reduce it to reduced row echelon form with the built-in rref.
   The solution column then reads off the last column, and the arithmetic is
   exact, so the printed solution is exact. The system below has the
   solution x = 1, y = 2, z = 3.

   Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/algebra/equations/three-linear-equations.epher"

   The knob to edit is the augmented matrix AM below.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob ----------------------------------------------------------------
// x + y + z = 6, 2x - y + z = 3, x + 2y - z = 2, as an augmented matrix
const AM = [[1, 1, 1, 6], [2, -1, 1, 3], [1, 2, -1, 2]]

// ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
R = rref(AM)                                // reduced row echelon form (echoes)
print("x:", R[1][4])
print("y:", R[2][4])
print("z:", R[3][4])
/* ---- expected output ----
= [[1, 1, 1, 6], [2, -1, 1, 3], [1, 2, -1, 2]]
= [[1, 0, 0, 1], [0, 1, 0, 2], [0, 0, 1, 3]]
= x: 1
= y: 2
= z: 3
*/
